[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10msqd = (dy2*dy2) - (2.0f*dx2*dy2) + (dx2*dx2) + (4.0f*dxy*dxy);
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
puts the [38;5;10m4*dxy*dxy[0m term in the [3mdestination[23m of the final [38;5;10maddss[0m, reversed from source order. LLVM picked the other order, so e.g. [38;5;10msrc = {0.0f, +inf, qNaN}[0m gave [38;5;10m7fc00000[0m from C but [38;5;10mffc00000[0m from Rust.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I fixed it by modelling the three SSE ops as explicit helpers ([38;5;10maddss[0m/[38;5;10msubss[0m/[38;5;10mmulss[0m) that reproduce the NaN-operand selection and quieting, then wiring the expression with the exact operand roles read off [38;5;10mobjdump -d[0m. Non-NaN operands fall through to the native [38;5;10mf32[0m op, so rounding — and the x86 [38;5;10m0xffc00000[0m indefinite QNaN from invalid ops like [38;5;10m0*inf[0m — is unchanged. [38;5;10msqrtf[0m is modelled on glibc's wrapper ([38;5;10misless(x,0)[0m is false for NaN, so NaN reaches [38;5;10mSQRTSS[0m and is quieted).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Behaviour was reproduced, not corrected: the [38;5;10m((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd)[0m clamp lets a NaN [38;5;10msqd[0m through unclamped (the comparison is unordered/false), and I kept that. The two branches of [38;5;10msrc[0] < src[1][0m compute an algebraically identical [38;5;10mlambda[0m and differ only in output slot order — I kept both arms rather than collapsing the test.[0m[0m
